<span id="S3.T1.4.10.2.2.1.1.1.1.1" class="ltx_p">Aes <math id="S3.T1.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\color[rgb]{0,0.5,0}5.649}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#008000;" mathcolor="#008000">5.649</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\color[rgb]{0,0.5,0}5.649}</annotation></semantics></math>, Rel <math id="S3.T1.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\color[rgb]{0,0.5,0}0.388}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#008000;" mathcolor="#008000">0.388</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\color[rgb]{0,0.5,0}0.388}</annotation></semantics></math></span>
</span></span></span>
</span></span><span id="S3.T1.4.10.2.3" class="ltx_text"></span></td>
<td id="S3.T1.4.10.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_bb" style="padding-left:2.8pt;padding-right:2.8pt;"><span id="S3.T1.4.10.3.1" class="ltx_text"></span><span id="S3.T1.4.10.3.2" class="ltx_text">
<span id="S3.T1.4.10.3.2.1" class="ltx_tabular ltx_align_middle">
<span id="S3.T1.4.10.3.2.1.1" class="ltx_tr">
<span id="S3.T1.4.10.3.2.1.1.1" class="ltx_td ltx_nopad_r ltx_align_left ltx_align_middle" style="padding-left:2.8pt;padding-right:2.8pt;">
<span id="S3.T1.4.10.3.2.1.1.1.1" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_align_middle" style="width:113.8pt;">
<span id="S3.T1.4.10.3.2.1.1.1.1.1" class="ltx_p">Aes <math id="S3.T1.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\color[rgb]{0.5,0,0}5.818}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#800000;" mathcolor="#800000">5.818</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\color[rgb]{0.5,0,0}5.818}</annotation></semantics></math>, Rel <math id="S3.T1.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\color[rgb]{0.5,0,0}0.437}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#800000;" mathcolor="#800000">0.437</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\color[rgb]{0.5,0,0}0.437}</annotation></semantics></math></span>
</span></span></span>
</span></span><span id="S3.T1.4.10.3.3" class="ltx_text"></span></td></tr>
</table>
</figure>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.3 </span>Training Framework</h3>

<div id="S3.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Score Discretization.</span> To simulate quality-controlled retrieval, we discretize the continuous quality scores of images into categorical levels that are more intuitive for users.
Given a score vector <math id="S3.SS3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{r}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝒓</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{r}</annotation></semantics></math> (either aesthetics <math id="S3.SS3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{s}^{a}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>𝒔</mi><mi>a</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{s}^{a}</annotation></semantics></math> or relevance <math id="S3.SS3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{s}^{r}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>𝒔</mi><mi>r</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{s}^{r}</annotation></semantics></math>),
each score <math id="S3.SS3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{r}_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝒓</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{r}_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> is mapped into one of three descriptive levels by partitioning the score distribution into three percentiles:<span id="footnote1" class="ltx_note ltx_role_footnote"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">1</sup><span class="ltx_note_outer"><span class="ltx_note_content"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">1</sup>
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_note">1</span>
              
              
              
            Our framework is general and supports arbitrary numbers of levels depending on the desired granularity. In Table <a href="#S4.T6" title="Table 6 ‣ 4.3 Quantitative Validation ‣ 4 Experiments ‣ Seeing Through Words: Controlling Visual Retrieval Quality with Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6</span></a>, Sec. <a href="#S4.SS5" title="4.5 Further Validation ‣ 4 Experiments ‣ Seeing Through Words: Controlling Visual Retrieval Quality with Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.5</span></a>, we provide an example with five quality levels.</span></span></span>:</p>
<table id="S3.E4" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="l(\bm{r}_{i})=\begin{cases}{\color[rgb]{0,0,0.5}\texttt{Low}},&amp;\bm{r}_{i}\leq\mathtt{perc}(\bm{r},\ p_{1}),\\
{\color[rgb]{0.5,0,0}\texttt{High}},&amp;\bm{r}_{i}&gt;\mathtt{perc}(\bm{r},\ p_{2}),\\
{\color[rgb]{0,0.5,0}\texttt{Medium}},&amp;\text{otherwise}.\end{cases}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>l</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝒓</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>{</mo><mtable columnspacing="5pt" displaystyle="true" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mtext style="--ltx-fg-color:#000080;" class="ltx_mathvariant_monospace" mathcolor="#000080">Low</mtext><mo>,</mo></mrow></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>𝒓</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mi>𝚙𝚎𝚛𝚌</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝒓</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mtext style="--ltx-fg-color:#800000;" class="ltx_mathvariant_monospace" mathcolor="#800000">High</mtext><mo>,</mo></mrow></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>𝒓</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>&gt;</mo><mrow><mi>𝚙𝚎𝚛𝚌</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝒓</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mtext style="--ltx-fg-color:#008000;" class="ltx_mathvariant_monospace" mathcolor="#008000">Medium</mtext><mo>,</mo></mrow></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mtext>otherwise</mtext><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">l(\bm{r}_{i})=\begin{cases}{\color[rgb]{0,0,0.5}\texttt{Low}},&amp;\bm{r}_{i}\leq\mathtt{perc}(\bm{r},\ p_{1}),\\
{\color[rgb]{0.5,0,0}\texttt{High}},&amp;\bm{r}_{i}&gt;\mathtt{perc}(\bm{r},\ p_{2}),\\
{\color[rgb]{0,0.5,0}\texttt{Medium}},&amp;\text{otherwise}.\end{cases}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(4)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS3.p1.2" class="ltx_p">Here, <math id="S3.SS3.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{r}_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝒓</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{r}_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> is the score of the <math id="S3.SS3.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math>-th sample and <math id="S3.SS3.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathtt{perc}(\bm{r},p)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝚙𝚎𝚛𝚌</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝒓</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>p</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathtt{perc}(\bm{r},p)</annotation></semantics></math> calculates the <math id="S3.SS3.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>%</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p\%</annotation></semantics></math> percentile of <math id="S3.SS3.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{r}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝒓</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{r}</annotation></semantics></math> as</p>
<table id="S3.E5" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathtt{perc}(\bm{r},p)=\widetilde{\bm{r}}[\lfloor\xi\rfloor]+(\xi-\lfloor\xi\rfloor)\cdot\big(\widetilde{\bm{r}}[\lfloor\xi\rfloor+1]-\widetilde{\bm{r}}[\lfloor\xi\rfloor]\big)," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>𝚙𝚎𝚛𝚌</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝒓</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>p</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>𝒓</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⌊</mo><mi>ξ</mi><mo stretchy="false">⌋</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>ξ</mi><mo>−</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⌊</mo><mi>ξ</mi><mo stretchy="false">⌋</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>𝒓</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⌊</mo><mi>ξ</mi><mo stretchy="false">⌋</mo></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>𝒓</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⌊</mo><mi>ξ</mi><mo stretchy="false">⌋</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathtt{perc}(\bm{r},p)=\widetilde{\bm{r}}[\lfloor\xi\rfloor]+(\xi-\lfloor\xi\rfloor)\cdot\big(\widetilde{\bm{r}}[\lfloor\xi\rfloor+1]-\widetilde{\bm{r}}[\lfloor\xi\rfloor]\big),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(5)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS3.p1.3" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S3.SS3.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\xi=\frac{p}{100}\cdot(n-1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ξ</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mfrac><mi>p</mi><mn>100</mn></mfrac><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\xi=\frac{p}{100}\cdot(n-1)</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S3.SS3.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widetilde{\bm{r}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>𝒓</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widetilde{\bm{r}}</annotation></semantics></math> is the sorted version of <math id="S3.SS3.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{r}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝒓</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{r}</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="S3.SS3.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lfloor\cdot\rfloor" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⌊</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo stretchy="false">⌋</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lfloor\cdot\rfloor</annotation></semantics></math> is the floor function.
Figure <a href="#S2.F1" title="Figure 1 ‣ 2.3 Theoretical Analysis ‣ 2 Preliminaries ‣ Seeing Through Words: Controlling Visual Retrieval Quality with Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a> illustrates the distributions of aesthetics and relevance scores and their discretized partitions.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS3.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS3.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS3.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Instruction Design.</span> We then train the completion function <math id="S3.SS3.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathtt{LLM}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝙻𝙻𝙼</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathtt{LLM}</annotation></semantics></math> on the augmented training set <math id="S3.SS3.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{D}=\{\mathcal{T},\bm{s}^{a},\bm{s}^{r}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>𝒔</mi><mi>a</mi></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>𝒔</mi><mi>r</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{D}=\{\mathcal{T},\bm{s}^{a},\bm{s}^{r}\}</annotation></semantics></math>.
The discretized quality levels serve as explicit conditions within instructions, enabling the <math id="S3.SS3.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathtt{LLM}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝙻𝙻𝙼</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathtt{LLM}</annotation></semantics></math> to generate quality-aware query completions.
For each image <math id="S3.SS3.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>I</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>, we design a concise instruction <math id="S3.SS3.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> of the following form:</p>
<table id="S3.Ex1" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex1.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext='\texttt{"Relevance:}\ l(\bm{s}^{r}_{i})\texttt{, Aesthetic:}\ l(\bm{s}^{a}_{i})\texttt{, Query:}\ \texttt{"}' display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_monospace">"Relevance:</mtext><mi>l</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>𝒔</mi><mi>i</mi><mi>r</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_monospace">, Aesthetic:</mtext><mi>l</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>𝒔</mi><mi>i</mi><mi>a</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_monospace">, Query:</mtext><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_monospace">"</mtext></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\texttt{"Relevance:}\ l(\bm{s}^{r}_{i})\texttt{, Aesthetic:}\ l(\bm{s}^{a}_{i})\texttt{, Query:}\ \texttt{"}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS3.p2.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S3.SS3.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="l(\bm{s}^{r}_{i})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>l</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>𝒔</mi><mi>i</mi><mi>r</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">l(\bm{s}^{r}_{i})</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS3.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="l(\bm{s}^{a}_{i})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>l</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>𝒔</mi><mi>i</mi><mi>a</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">l(\bm{s}^{a}_{i})</annotation></semantics></math> represent the categorical quality levels defined in Eq. (<a href="#S3.E4" title="In 3.3 Training Framework ‣ 3 Quality-Conditioned Query Completion ‣ Seeing Through Words: Controlling Visual Retrieval Quality with Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a>).
During training, this instruction provides a lightweight yet effective mechanism to condition query generation on the specified quality preferences.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS3.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS3.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS3.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Model Training.</span> To stimulate the quality control process during model training, we use the descriptive levels <math id="S3.SS3.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="l(\bm{s}^{r}_{i})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>l</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>𝒔</mi><mi>i</mi><mi>r</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">l(\bm{s}^{r}_{i})</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS3.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="l(\bm{s}^{a}_{i})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>l</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>𝒔</mi><mi>i</mi><mi>a</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">l(\bm{s}^{a}_{i})</annotation></semantics></math> of image <math id="S3.SS3.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>I</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> as the quality conditions, which are incorporated into the instruction <math id="S3.SS3.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>.
We then concatenate instructions <math id="S3.SS3.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> with the textual description <math id="S3.SS3.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> for each image <math id="S3.SS3.p3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>I</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>, and then train the proposed QCQC model with the standard autoregressive next-token prediction loss.
In this way, our QCQC model learns to generate query completions that are not only semantically relevant but also controllable according to the given quality constraints.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS3.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS3.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS3.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Inference Strategy.</span>
During the inference stage, we concatenate a similar instruction with each testing query. To simulate user preferences, we evaluate various relevance-aesthetic combinations, such as “low relevance, low aesthetic” and “high relevance, high aesthetic”.
Then the model generates completed queries based on the instructions, testing queries, and specified quality conditions.
For efficient similarity search on large-scale galleries, we utilize the <math id="S3.SS3.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathtt{FAISS}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝙵𝙰𝙸𝚂𝚂</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathtt{FAISS}</annotation></semantics></math> library <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib8" title="" class="ltx_ref">Johnson et al., 2019</a>)</cite> to identify the nearest images for the queries.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S3.T2" class="ltx_table">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table">Table 2: </span>Query completions with their retrieved images and quality scores on <span id="S3.T2.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">Flickr2.4M</span></figcaption>
